-- ============================================================================
-- FC5 ASSOCIATE DEEP SEED: Relief & Flare Systems, beginner tier.
--
-- Six modules, 26 lessons, 132 questions (15 per module bank plus a 42
-- question final exam). The structure row is GENERATED from the tier's
-- manifest.json rather than hand typed.
--
-- What this tier owns: THE FOUR API 520 ROUTES. What a relief valve is sized
-- for and the units this engine speaks; gas and vapour, with the critical
-- ratio, the coefficient C and the subcritical branch the back pressure
-- decides; liquid, with the viscosity correction and the loop that closes on
-- itself; steam, with the Napier correction and the superheat factor the
-- caller types; the step from a required area to an orifice letter on the
-- API 526 ladder; and the Associate reading walked end to end.
--
-- THE ENGINE FACT THIS TIER OWNS. Every route here is a closed form, and the
-- back pressure picks the gas branch. Above the critical ratio the engine uses
-- F2 and IGNORES Kb entirely, so a reader who types a bellows factor into a
-- subcritical case has typed a number that changes nothing.
--
-- NOT DERIVED HERE, taught as a limit and never graded in any tier: six items
-- HELD FOR LITERATURE (the Kv fit coefficients, the sphere-drag correlation,
-- the Napier boundaries, the pool-fire constants and exponent, the customary
-- allowable intensities, and whether the standard prints 1.15 or four
-- thirds), two TYPED published inputs (Kb, Kw and KSH; the API 526 table) and
-- one stated LIMIT the caller applies (the 25 ft wetted height). The capstone
-- grades none of them: the liquid case is inviscid so no Kv constant enters,
-- the valve is conventional so Kb is 1 by the standard, and the steam case sits
-- far below the Napier threshold so KN is exactly 1.
-- ============================================================================

